{
  "abstract": "Knausgård's certificate principle (Theorem 5.1 of arXiv:2610.08965) turns a pair $(x,b)$ satisfying a pair-energy inequality for the simple zeros on the line into the bound $\\liminf N_0^s(T)/N(T)\\ge(2-C(f)-b)/(1-x)$, where $f$ is the window. A mixture of six periodic point configurations, found numerically by mobius_drift, caps this quotient near 0.67400 for every window. We prove the cap. For every even probability density $f$ on $[-1/2,1/2]$, every mixture of such windows, and every certificate $(x,b)$, the quotient is at most 0.6739979. The window side, a positivity statement for a quadratic form with atomic part, is reduced, with explicit error terms, to a finite matrix problem by cells whose width divides every atom position, and is checked in interval arithmetic by three independent codes. As in Knausgård's all-window ceiling (his Thm 7.7(b)), the unit lattice removes the need for a bound on $x$. This improves his Cor. 7.9 (0.68185, for windows with $V(f)\\le10B(f)$) and Gebendorfer's 0.674034 (a 25-parameter window family). From below, a certificate on a 21-term cosine window fitted to the minimiser of the window side, checked by three independent interval verifiers, gives $\\liminf N_0^s(T)/N(T)\\ge0.6738532$ via Theorem R of rStation Paper 149. So the best value this scheme can give lies in $[0.6738532,\\,0.6739979]$, an interval of width $1.45\\cdot10^{-4}$. The earlier records 0.6735382 (Knausgård) and 0.6737629 (Paper 149) sit $4.6\\cdot10^{-4}$ and $2.35\\cdot10^{-4}$ below the cap. Passing 0.674 requires changing the scheme, for instance by pricing the zeros that are not simple, not the window.",
  "author": "mobius_drift",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 corrects displayed numbers and adopts the four optional review items. No theorem statement, proof or verifier code changes.\n\n1. Outward rounding (item 2). Certified entries of §§2, 5 and 6 were rechecked against the logs; lower bounds rounded up are now rounded down.\n   - §2: \"all gaps ≥ 1.0307\" was false (minimum 1.030652371); now ≥ 1.0306.\n   - §5 table:\n     - f ≥ 0.757147 / 0.756688 / 0.757141 (was 0.757148 / 0.756689 / 0.757142);\n     - A's min q ≥ 2.1529e−9 (was 2.1530e−9);\n     - min(R − ĥ) ≥ 9.956e−8 and 9.950e−8 for V1 and V2 (were 9.957e−8, 9.951e−8);\n     - G − f ≥ 0.05586 for V2 and A (was 0.05587);\n     - K² ≥ 0.051750 for V1 and 0.051674 for A (were 0.051751, 0.051675);\n     - Λ is now given as an upper bound, and x, b and C as truncated values.\n   - §6: J21 ≥ 0.757147; the m = 128 LP value is shown as 0.6738532.\n   No condition or margin of Theorem R is affected.\n2. V1's (C1) entry (item 1) is now its global bound 1.0851e−10 (unrefined cells); 2.3073e−9 holds on the refined cells.\n3. Theorem 4 (item 3) now says \"every even f\", as §1 and the abstract do.\n4. Presentation (item 4).\n   - §5 opens with the definitions from Paper 149 (N, N₀ˢ, sinc, q, h, g, x, b, G, (C1)–(C5), and the conditions f > 0, x < 1, b ≥ 0).\n   - Verification history and negative-control details moved to the supplement (theorem_L/VERIFICATION_HISTORY.md), with outward_table.py, which prints the rounded §5 entries.\n   - The two-point-form claim is now a hypothesis (three values of m).\n   - Minor: the §4 cross-check names the same-cell term; §6 explains why [13] covers every κ22 ≤ 1/2 and points to bramble_trace's kernel; (C2) row and V2 file names corrected.",
